        <Button>Save</Button>
        <ThemeContext value="dark">
          <Button>Always dark</Button>
        </ThemeContext>
        <button onClick={() => setTheme(t => (t === "light" ? "dark" : "light"))}>Toggle</button>
      </ThemeContext>
    </UserContext>
  );
}

concepts.body:
As a component grows, state updates get scattered across many handlers. A **reducer** gathers them into one pure function that takes the current state and an *action* (an object describing what happened) and returns the next state:

```jsx
function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "added":   return [...state, { id: action.id, text: action.text, done: false }];
    case "deleted": return state.filter(t => t.id !== action.id);
    default: throw new Error("Unknown action: " + action.type);
  }
}

const [tasks, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialTasks);
dispatch({ type: "added", id: 3, text: "Write docs" });
```

Event handlers now describe **what happened** (`dispatch({ type: "deleted", id })`) and the reducer decides **how state changes**. The reducer receives the current state and the action and returns the next state.

Rules for reducers:

- They must be **pure**: no requests, timers or mutations; return new objects instead of modifying `state`. (Strict Mode calls them twice to check.)
- Each action describes one user interaction, even if it changes several fields. `"reset_form"` beats five `"set_field"` actions.
- Name actions in the past tense or as events (`"added"`, `"item_selected"`).

When to choose `useReducer` over `useState`: several pieces of state change together, the next state depends on the previous in non-trivial ways, or you want to check the logic on its own with a **unit test**, an automated check of one small piece of code (a reducer is just a function). For a boolean or a single input, `useState` is simpler.

`dispatch` is the same function on every render, so it's safe to pass far down the tree; pairing a reducer with context is a lightweight way to share app-wide state. A third argument, `init`, computes the initial state only on the first render: `useReducer(reducer, arg, init)`.

concepts.code:
import { useReducer } from "react";

function cartReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "added": {
      const existing = state.items.find(i => i.id === action.item.id);
      const items = existing
        ? state.items.map(i => (i.id === action.item.id ? { ...i, qty: i.qty + 1 } : i))
        : [...state.items, { ...action.item, qty: 1 }];
      return { ...state, items };
    }
    case "removed":
      return { ...state, items: state.items.filter(i => i.id !== action.id) };
    case "coupon_applied":
      return { ...state, discount: action.percent };
    default:
      throw new Error(`Unknown action ${action.type}`);
  }
}

export default function Cart() {
  const [cart, dispatch] = useReducer(cartReducer, { items: [], discount: 0 });
  const total = cart.items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price * i.qty, 0) * (1 - cart.discount / 100);
  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: "added", item: { id: 1, name: "Pen", price: 2 } })}>Add pen</button>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: "coupon_applied", percent: 10 })}>SAVE10</button>
      <p>{cart.items.length} lines, total {total.toFixed(2)}</p>
    </>
  );
}

concepts.body:
The basic approach in the browser fetches in an Effect whose dependency array holds the inputs:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  let ignore = false;
  fetchUser(id).then(u => { if (!ignore) setUser(u); });
  return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [id]);
```

The `ignore` flag matters. If `id` changes quickly, responses can arrive out of order; without the flag, a slow old response overwrites the new one (a **race condition**: the result depends on which of two things happens to finish first). Another way is an `AbortController`, a browser object that cancels a `fetch`: cancel in the cleanup, and ignore the `AbortError` the cancelled request throws. You also need loading and error states, usually one `status` value that is `"loading"`, `"success"` or `"error"`, so the component is always in exactly one of them.

Hand-written Effect fetching has real limits. Nothing is cached, so going back to a screen loads it all again. Two components asking for the same data send two requests. And when a parent fetches and renders, and only then its child starts fetching, the waits add up one after another: a **waterfall**. Real apps usually use one of these:

- A **data library** such as TanStack Query or SWR: `useQuery({ queryKey: ["user", id], queryFn })` handles caching, sharing one request between components, refreshing in the background, trying failed requests again, and loading/error state for you.
- A **framework's** own data loading: React Router and Next.js can load a screen's data on the server, before or while it renders, so nothing waits in a chain.
- React 19's `use(promise)` with Suspense (both covered at the Advanced level): a component reads a promise, and React shows a loading placeholder until it settles. The promise must come from a cache or a parent, not be created in the same component's render, or it would be new every time.

Wrap the logic in a custom Hook (`useUser(id)`) so you can swap implementations later. Requests that change data (saving, deleting) don't belong in Effects at all: send them from event handlers.

concepts.code:
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

function useJson(url) {
  const [state, setState] = useState({ status: "loading", data: null, error: null });

  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    setState({ status: "loading", data: null, error: null });

    (async () => {
      try {
        const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
        setState({ status: "success", data: await res.json(), error: null });
      } catch (error) {
        if (error.name !== "AbortError") setState({ status: "error", data: null, error });
      }
    })();

    return () => controller.abort();   // stale requests never win the race
  }, [url]);

  return state;
}

export default function User({ id }) {
  const { status, data, error } = useJson(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/${id}`);
  if (status === "loading") return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (status === "error") return <p>Failed: {error.message}</p>;
  return <h2>{data.name}</h2>;
}

